#!/usr/bin/env bash
# Run the verifications that are cheap enough to have no excuse, before anything leaves this machine.
#
# ## Why a hook and not a habit
#
# This project has eight verifications and only two ran automatically: `lint.yml` (lint, typecheck, tests)
# and `capture-regression.yml`, which is path-filtered. The other six needed a human to remember, and the
# record of what that produces is unambiguous:
#
#   - `capture-check.mjs` was required after any change to `capture-core.mjs` and had never been run once,
#     across many such changes, because running it meant stopping the worker on the guest.
#   - `release:gate` was broken from the day it was written: it invoked the acceptance evaluator with no
#     --data, so stability could not be measured at all, and said "not measured OR unstable" in one string.
#   - The acceptance gate sat FAILING while three other gates were green, and nobody was looking.
#
# So this is the same rule the rest of the repo already follows for worker VMs, the page server and NVDA:
# anything a human has to remember is something that does not happen. Automate it or lose it.
#
# ## Why these five and not the others
#
# Measured: all five together take about FIVE SECONDS. They need no Windows worker, no Codex login and no
# network, so there is no tradeoff to weigh and nothing to opt out of on a normal day.
#
# Deliberately NOT here: `gate:stability`, `evidence:check` and `capture:check` need a running worker;
# `eval:gate` needs a local Codex login and ~75 minutes at EVAL_RUNS=3. Those are release-time gates
# (`npm run release:gate`), not push-time ones. Putting a 75-minute check on `git push` would get the hook
# deleted within a day, which is worse than not having it.
#
# Override, for a work-in-progress branch or when something below is broken for unrelated reasons:
#   A11Y_SKIP_VERIFY_REASON="<why>" A11Y_SKIP_VERIFY=1 git push ...   # the reason is required
#
# ## The FAST/FULL split, added 2026-09-06
#
# Six agents pushing after every commit (chairman's rule) multiplied the heaviest thing this repo runs by
# the number of agents: each push ran the full suite, and `npm test` spawns a tsx worker per test file, so
# several full suites at once starved the host of memory. `.github/workflows/lint.yml` was widened the same
# day to run on every `agent/**`/`lead/**` push -- CI now has somewhere to run the full suite that isn't
# this laptop, which is what makes shrinking the LOCAL hook for a branch push safe rather than a silent
# reduction in what gets checked.
#
# `main` pushes are UNCHANGED: full suite, every time, because nothing else gates a merge to `main`.
# `agent/*`/`lead/*` pushes run a FAST gate instead — lint, typecheck (both whole-repo checks regardless of
# branch, so neither shrinks), and unit tests SCOPED to the packages/<name> directories the branch actually
# touched against `origin/main` (`scripts/changed-packages.mjs`). A push touching no `packages/` file at
# all (docs, this hook, the workflow) runs every package's tests -- an EMPTY touched-package result must
# read as "run everything", never as "run nothing", the same rule this repo's discovery tests already
# apply to an empty walk.
#
# THIS IS DELIBERATELY NOT A REPLACEMENT FOR THE FULL SUITE. A branch that breaks a package it never
# touched will pass this gate and fail CI -- by design, not by gap: building a reliable cross-package
# dependency graph to close that gap is a real project of its own, and a wrong graph is a guard answering
# about the wrong population, the exact class closed elsewhere today (docs/backlog.md). CI is the real gate
# for a branch now; this hook is the fast, local first pass.
#
# ## UPDATE 2026-09-06: CI moved from the branch push to the branch's PR, then off `push` entirely
#
# `lint.yml` (above) is retired, replaced by `.github/workflows/ci.yml`. Everything above this paragraph
# is still literally true of the LOCAL hook -- the fast/full split, the scoping, the "empty means
# everything" rule -- and none of it changed. What changed is WHERE the full suite the fast gate hands off
# to actually runs, TWICE in one day: first to `ci.yml` triggering on `pull_request` and on
# `push: branches: [main]`, then (chairman's direction, same day) to `pull_request` ONLY -- a check that
# runs after a merge cannot stop it, so `push` is not merely scoped to `main` now, it is ABSENT. That is
# safe rather than a silent reduction because every unit's workflow ends with `gh pr create` immediately
# after pushing (the standing worker-loop flow), and branch protection requires the PR's checks green AND
# the branch up to date with `main` before the dispatcher may merge -- so the commit CI tested is the
# commit that lands, via a squash or fast-forward, never a merge commit CI never saw. A branch push with no
# PR yet open gets only this hook; a branch push followed by its PR gets this hook AND the full `ci.yml`
# run, on the PR, before anything reaches `main` at all.
#
# `A11Y_TEST_CONCURRENCY=4` bounded a single suite's own tsx worker count on this host. It is GONE below,
# not stale-but-kept: its only call site (`npm test` on main) went with the UPDATE right below.
#
# ## UPDATE 2026-09-07: package test suites moved out of this hook entirely
#
# ceo's ruling: CI's acceptance job (#353, unit 2 of the pipeline) now actually RUNS a PR's stated
# acceptance command against real code on every PR, on a fork's own read-only checkout -- so a local
# `npm test` here duplicated a check the pipeline already makes, on every push, before this hook could
# even finish. Measured on this checkout, warm, FAST-gate branch touching one package: BEFORE (lint,
# typecheck, one package's `npx tsx --test`) 3m 27.85s wall (207.85s); AFTER (lint, typecheck, the mjs
# parse check below) 13.94s wall -- a ~14.9x reduction. RE-MEASURED 2026-09-09 on this host at load 6.56:
# lint 3s, typecheck 5s, mjs parse 2s, TOTAL 10s -- the AFTER figure holds two days on, and both numbers
# are kept because a reader who takes the BEFORE one for the current cost concludes this hook is worth
# skipping. One did, nine times, the same day. The unit tests were the overwhelming majority of
# that time; lint and typecheck alone are `npm run --silent lint`/`typecheck`'s own per-package
# `tsc --build`, which this hook was never trying to avoid.
#
# ONE test file survives, by name, not as a package suite: `mjs-parses.test.ts` (packages/worker-fleet/
# src/) is the check CLAUDE.md itself names as "the only real check" for a `.mjs` file that lint and
# `tsc --noEmit` cannot see fail -- a backtick inside a template literal holding PowerShell or shell
# script, which broke TWICE in one session before this test existed. CI's `ci.yml` runs the FULL suite
# (which includes this file) on every PR, so on a branch WITH an open PR it is redundant there too -- but
# a branch push with no PR yet (the common case: this hook runs, THEN `gh pr create`) has nothing else
# checking it before the code leaves this machine, and it is one test, not a package's worth.
#
# The corpus-gated block below (`check-signals`, `rules:gate`) is UNCHANGED and deliberately so: CI
# structurally cannot run either one (`runs/` is gitignored, so a GitHub-hosted runner has no corpus to
# read), so nothing else in the pipeline covers what they cover. Cutting them here would not move the
# check to CI, it would delete it.
# ## UPDATE 2026-09-11 (#911): three checks, and everything above this line is history
#
# Step 4 of the CI Reset, chairman-approved. **This hook is a courtesy, not a gate. CI is the gate**, and
# everything here that CI also runs was a second copy of it minutes earlier. What remains:
#
#   - **lint**, scoped to the changed PATHS (not the changed packages -- see the call site for why the
#     distinction is load-bearing rather than pedantic);
#   - **typecheck**, the whole program, because tsc's unit IS the program -- again, see the call site;
#   - **the leak scan**, `tracked-source-leak-guard.test.ts` and `tracked-prose-leak-guard.test.ts`,
#     the one check here whose value is being BEFORE the push rather than before the merge. This
#     repository is public; a pushed branch is visible the moment it lands.
#
# THREE CLAIMS ABOVE ARE NOW FALSE, and they are named rather than quietly left to be re-read as current:
#
#   1. **The FAST/FULL split is gone.** One path, whatever the branch. The board-only path is gone with
#      it -- and it had a hole: it never ran the sweep, so it never ran the leak guards, on the one kind
#      of diff (`docs/board/summaries/*.md`) made entirely of the prose they scan.
#   2. **`mjs-parses.test.ts` no longer runs here.** CI runs the full suite on every PR. It was kept for
#      the window between a branch push and its PR; that window is now covered by nothing local, which is
#      the same trade every other check here just took.
#   3. **The corpus-gated block is gone, and the paragraph arguing to keep it is superseded.** That
#      paragraph said cutting `check-signals`/`rules:gate` here would delete the check rather than move
#      it. The 2026-09-06 ruling answers it: a gate that reads `runs/` gives a VERDICT only when the
#      agent driving the fleet and the lab runs it -- anyone else may run one as a PRE-CHECK, never as a
#      reported result. So this hook never held that check to begin with; it held a pre-check, and the
#      authoritative one lives on the lab. What replaces it is an UNCONDITIONAL line naming both checks
#      and where the verdict comes from -- unconditional because the old form was silent on exactly the
#      machine that has a stale `runs/`, which is the machine that most needs telling.
#
# Measured on this checkout at `40e36ba4`, wall clock: the tree-wide sweep (22 files, 177 tests) 30.22s;
# lint 3.93s; typecheck 4.86s; the two leak guards together 1.39s. So the hook goes from about 39s to
# about 10s, and 75% of that saving is the sweep alone.
#
# `pre-push-hook-scope.test.ts` is what holds this to three: it enumerates this file's own `run` call
# sites and every npm/node invocation outside them, and a fourth check fails it. The git-only refusals
# below -- the stale-base check, resolve-toward-main, the 300-deletion warning, the armed-PR lookup and
# the A11Y_SKIP_VERIFY gate -- are outside that assertion by name: none is a copy of CI, each costs
# milliseconds, and they are push SAFETY rather than verification.
set -euo pipefail

# A BYPASS NEEDS A REASON, AND THE REASON IS PRINTED -- the same shape as A11Y_STALE_BASE_REASON,
# A11Y_RESOLVE_REASON and A11Y_PRIMARY_CHECKOUT_REASON below and in `post-checkout`. This block used to
# accept a bare `A11Y_SKIP_VERIFY=1` and print one general sentence.
#
# MEASURED 2026-09-09, and by the session that maintains this hook. Nine pushes went out with
# `A11Y_SKIP_VERIFY=1`, on the belief that this hook costs three and a half minutes and the host was at
# load 15-35.
#
# THAT BELIEF WAS WRONG, AND THE HEADER ABOVE ALREADY SAID SO. The 2026-09-07 note records "BEFORE
# (lint, typecheck, one package's tsx --test) 3m 27.85s; AFTER (lint, typecheck, the mjs parse check)
# 13.94s" -- one sentence carrying both, and the BEFORE half was read as the current cost. Re-measured
# 2026-09-09 at load 6.56 on this host: lint 3s, typecheck 5s, mjs parse 2s, TOTAL 10s. So the thing
# being skipped to save time cost ten seconds, and the substitutes cost four CI rounds.
#
# It printed "nothing was verified" every time and was scrolled past every time, and three of those pushes carried
# `tsc` errors this hook's own `npm run typecheck` reports -- verified by restoring one defect from a
# copy and running the hook's exact command, which exits 2 and names the file. Two PRs sat BEHIND AND RED
# on the queue table as a result. **Four CI rounds and three red PRs, for the minutes saved.**
#
# So the general disclaimer is replaced by two things a reader cannot scroll past:
#
#   - THE LIST of what is being skipped, by name. "nothing was verified" is true and abstract; "lint,
#     typecheck and the leak scan" is three specific things you are choosing not to do -- and after #911
#     the third one is the reason this matters more than it used to: the leak scan is the only check here
#     that cannot be caught up by CI, because the push it would have stopped has already happened.
#   - A REASON, required. A bare `=1` can be inherited from an exported variable, a wrapper script, or a
#     habit from an hour ago; a sentence has to be written by whoever is pushing, now, and it lands in
#     the log rather than in somebody's memory.
# BEGIN SKIP-VERIFY NEEDS A REASON
if [ "${A11Y_SKIP_VERIFY:-0}" = "1" ]; then
  if [ -z "${A11Y_SKIP_VERIFY_REASON:-}" ]; then
    echo "pre-push: REFUSING to skip -- A11Y_SKIP_VERIFY=1 no longer skips on its own." >&2
    echo "" >&2
    echo "It would skip: lint, typecheck and the leak scan." >&2
    echo "" >&2
    echo "If that is deliberate: A11Y_SKIP_VERIFY_REASON=\"<why>\" A11Y_SKIP_VERIFY=1 git push ..." >&2
    echo "The reason is printed, not just the override, so a deliberate exception is in the log rather" >&2
    echo "than in somebody's memory -- and a bypass cannot be inherited silently from an exported" >&2
    echo "variable. #702: three pushes carried tsc errors this hook reports, past a bare =1." >&2
    exit 1
  fi
  echo "pre-push: SKIPPED -- lint, typecheck and the leak scan did NOT run." >&2
  echo "pre-push: overridden: $A11Y_SKIP_VERIFY_REASON" >&2
  exit 0
fi
# END SKIP-VERIFY NEEDS A REASON

cd "$(git rev-parse --show-toplevel)"

# BELT AND BRACES. git exports GIT_DIR/GIT_WORK_TREE/GIT_INDEX_FILE into this hook's own environment, and
# `npm test` inherits it — which is exactly how a test that spawned git with `cwd` alone and an inherited
# `env` forged 15 commits into THIS repo on 2026-09-06 (docs/backlog.md, "a closed row created the
# exposure"). `scripts/test-support/git-sandbox.ts` is the primary defence, applied per test; this is the
# second, independent one `orchestrator` required: even a future test that bypasses the helper entirely
# cannot reach this repository through an inherited GIT_DIR, because by the time `npm test` runs there is
# none left to inherit. Scrubbed AFTER `git rev-parse --show-toplevel` above (which correctly needs
# GIT_DIR to resolve THIS hook's own repo) and before anything that runs test code.
for v in "${!GIT_@}"; do unset "$v"; done

failed=()
skipped=()

# #348: A FAILED CHECKOUT IN AN && CHAIN LEAVES A BRANCH THAT LOOKS RIGHT. `git checkout -q main &&
# git reset --hard origin/main` silently no-ops past the `&&` when the checkout fails (e.g. `main` is
# already checked out in another worktree) -- the reset never runs, and a branch built off that stale
# HEAD is a valid commit on a plausibly-named branch. Measured cost of the real incident this reproduces:
# 65 files, 3,281 deletions, caught only by hand (`c7b1a0a0` is the surviving artefact). Twice in one day,
# by two independent sessions, and `ceo` hit the identical trap with `git checkout -B`.
#
# TWO DOTS, not three. `git diff --stat origin/main...HEAD` reports only what HEAD added since the merge
# base -- which on a stale branch IS the stale point, so it prints a clean, reassuring summary for
# exactly the incident that motivated this check. `git merge-base --is-ancestor origin/main HEAD` is the
# real test and needs no dot count; the two-dot diff below is a second, human-readable signal, never a
# substitute for the ancestry check.
#
# A stale LOCAL `origin/main` (this hook does not fetch) makes the ancestry check pass on a branch that
# is genuinely behind -- the guard reassuring you for the reason it exists. Fetch before trusting a green
# result here, the same caveat every other `origin/main`-relative check in this hook already carries.
#
# BEGIN #348 STALE-BASE CHECK -- extraction markers for pre-push-stale-base.test.ts; keep both if edited.
#
# #583: A DELETION HAS NO BASE TO BE STALE AGAINST. `git push origin --delete <branch>` sends this hook
# no commits at all -- there is nothing being pushed for "does HEAD contain origin/main's tip" to be a
# question ABOUT. The refusal, and its own advice ("merge origin/main in before pushing"), name an action
# that does not exist for a deletion. git's own pre-push protocol (githooks(5)) reports exactly this on
# STDIN, one line per ref update, as `<local ref> SP <local sha1> SP <remote ref> SP <remote sha1>` -- a
# deleted ref's LOCAL sha1 is the all-zeros object name. Read here, once, rather than inferred from HEAD,
# which names the CURRENT checkout's branch and has nothing to do with which ref is actually being
# pushed. A push mixing a deletion with a real update still has a real update line present, so this only
# ever skips when EVERY line read is a deletion -- and an empty read (no lines at all) must NOT count as
# "all deletions", since that is indistinguishable from a caller that supplied no stdin and existed before
# this check did; both keep this check fully active.
push_ref_lines=0
push_deletion_lines=0
while IFS=' ' read -r push_local_ref push_local_sha push_remote_ref push_remote_sha; do
  push_ref_lines=$((push_ref_lines + 1))
  if [ "$push_local_sha" = "0000000000000000000000000000000000000000" ]; then
    push_deletion_lines=$((push_deletion_lines + 1))
  fi
done
push_is_all_deletions=0
if [ "$push_ref_lines" -gt 0 ] && [ "$push_ref_lines" -eq "$push_deletion_lines" ]; then
  push_is_all_deletions=1
fi

if [ "$push_is_all_deletions" = "1" ]; then
  skipped+=("stale-base-check (this push only deletes remote ref(s), which have no base to be stale against)")
elif git rev-parse --verify -q origin/main >/dev/null; then
  if ! git merge-base --is-ancestor origin/main HEAD; then
    if [ -n "${A11Y_STALE_BASE_REASON:-}" ]; then
      echo "pre-push: HEAD does not contain origin/main's tip, overridden: $A11Y_STALE_BASE_REASON" >&2
    else
      echo "pre-push: REFUSING — HEAD does not contain origin/main's tip (#348: a failed checkout in an" >&2
      echo "&& chain leaves a branch that looks right). Merge origin/main in before pushing." >&2
      echo "If this is deliberate: A11Y_STALE_BASE_REASON=\"<why>\" git push ... — the reason is printed," >&2
      echo "not just the override, so a deliberate exception is in the log rather than in somebody's" >&2
      echo "memory." >&2
      exit 1
    fi
  fi
  # PRINTED, never refused: a legitimate large deletion exists (a purge, a revert); a SILENT one does
  # not. `tail -1` reads the diffstat's own summary line, e.g. "3 files changed, 1 insertion(+), 3281
  # deletion(-)" -- absent when nothing was deleted, which `grep -oE` then reports as no match rather
  # than a false zero.
  stale_base_deletions="$(git diff --stat origin/main..HEAD 2>/dev/null | tail -1 \
    | grep -oE '[0-9]+ deletion' | grep -oE '[0-9]+' || true)"
  if [ -n "$stale_base_deletions" ] && [ "$stale_base_deletions" -gt 300 ]; then
    echo "pre-push: this push deletes $stale_base_deletions line(s) against origin/main (two-dot diff)" >&2
    echo "-- worth a second look before it lands." >&2
  fi
else
  skipped+=("stale-base-check (origin/main not resolvable here)")
fi
# END #348 STALE-BASE CHECK

# BEGIN B5 RESOLVE-TOWARD-MAIN -- extraction markers for pre-push-resolve-toward-main.test.ts.
#
# ONE RESOLUTION THAT KEPT ITS OWN SIDE REMOVED FOUR MERGED UNITS AND PASSED EVERY CHECK -- #232, and it
# is the reason this block exists. A branch merged `main` in, answered the conflicts with its own side,
# and deleted #363, #183, a schema-migration unit and a workspace-scope unit on the way through. Source
# and tests went together, CONSISTENTLY, so nothing dangled: every test passed, `gate` was green, and the
# loss was invisible for hours.
#
# ## THE PATH LIST IS PRINTED. THE SYMBOL CHECK IS WHAT REFUSES.
#
# A path list cannot tell a legitimate edit from a wholesale revert -- `M check-real-page-findings.ts`
# looks identical either way, and that file was the centre of #232. Worse, a path list cannot see a
# resolution that keeps BOTH sides: A5's merge concatenated two hunks sharing a trailing `});` into one
# unterminated function, and BOTH diffs were clean because no path changed. A syntax error is not a
# deleted path.
#
# So the question this asks is CONTENT: does every symbol `main` exports from a file this branch touched
# still resolve after the merge? No list to maintain, and it answers for the two cases a path list cannot.
#
# ## IT PRINTS THE SIZE OF THE SET IT EXAMINED
#
# A guard reporting "nothing lost" over an empty set is worse than no guard, because #232 proved the
# failure is silent -- nobody goes looking. So the count is printed whether or not anything is wrong, and
# a zero population is stated rather than read as a pass.
#
# ## RUN ONLY WHEN `main` IS AN ANCESTOR, which the block above has already established
#
# On a branch that has not merged `main`, every symbol `main` gained since would read as LOST -- the
# same false-positive flood that makes the two-dot path diff useless there. The refusal above is this
# check's precondition, not merely its neighbour.
if git rev-parse --verify -q origin/main >/dev/null && git merge-base --is-ancestor origin/main HEAD; then
  resolve_checked=0
  resolve_lost=()
  # `git diff --name-only` on the two-dot form: files that DIFFER from main, which is the population a
  # revert lives in. A file identical to main's cannot have dropped anything.
  while IFS= read -r changed_file; do
    case "$changed_file" in *.ts|*.mjs|*.js) ;; *) continue ;; esac
    git cat-file -e "origin/main:$changed_file" 2>/dev/null || continue   # new file: nothing to lose
    # `|| true` BECAUSE grep EXITS 1 ON NO MATCH, and under `set -o pipefail` that status becomes the
    # assignment's, which `set -e` then treats as a failure and kills the hook on. A file with no exports
    # is the ordinary case -- every test file in the repository -- so without this the guard aborts the
    # push on the first such file, silently, having checked almost nothing. Second instance of this shape
    # in one block: a command whose "found nothing" is indistinguishable from its "went wrong".
    exported="$(git show "origin/main:$changed_file" \
      | grep -oE '^export (async )?(function|const|class) [A-Za-z0-9_]+' \
      | awk '{print $NF}' | sort -u || true)"
    # `if`, NOT `[ -z ... ] && continue`. Under `set -e` a `&&` list whose LAST command does not run
    # takes the condition's non-zero status and kills the hook -- so with a non-empty `$exported` the
    # whole push died silently, mid-loop, with no message. A guard that aborts the hook is worse than no
    # guard: it fails every push for a reason that looks like nothing at all.
    if [ -z "$exported" ]; then continue; fi
    # A FILE, NEVER A PIPE -- `printf '%s' "$after" | grep -q` REFUSED A PUSH OVER SYMBOLS THAT WERE THERE.
    # `grep -q` exits at its FIRST match, so `printf` is killed by SIGPIPE (141) whenever the file is
    # larger than the pipe buffer and the match lands early -- and `set -o pipefail` above makes 141 the
    # PIPELINE's status, which is indistinguishable here from "not found". Measured 2026-09-09 on
    # `real-page-corpus.mjs` (73 KB, `assertDisjoint` first at byte 1878): a REFUSAL naming two symbols
    # sitting in plain sight, and a NONDETERMINISTIC one -- three consecutive runs of the same loop over
    # the same file flagged three different sets, because whether `printf` finishes writing before `grep`
    # exits is a race against a 64 KB kernel buffer.
    #
    # It can only ever refuse WRONGLY, never pass wrongly: a genuinely absent symbol makes grep read to
    # EOF, so printf always completes and the status is grep's own 1. That asymmetry is the worse of the
    # two -- a guard that refuses correct pushes is one people learn to override, which is how
    # `A11Y_SKIP_VERIFY=1` came to be used six times in one evening.
    #
    # The general form is this file's own `| tail` lesson pointed at the WRITER rather than the reader:
    # under `pipefail`, never put a large writer upstream of a reader that exits early. Third instance of
    # a status-that-is-not-the-command's in this block alone, after the two `set -e` notes above.
    after_file="$(mktemp)"
    git show "HEAD:$changed_file" > "$after_file" 2>/dev/null || true
    while IFS= read -r symbol; do
      if [ -z "$symbol" ]; then continue; fi
      resolve_checked=$((resolve_checked + 1))
      grep -q "\b${symbol}\b" "$after_file" || resolve_lost+=("$changed_file :: $symbol")
    done <<< "$exported"
    rm -f "$after_file"
  done < <(git diff --name-only origin/main..HEAD)

  echo "pre-push: resolve-toward-main — $resolve_checked exported symbol(s) checked across the files this" >&2
  echo "branch changes; ${#resolve_lost[@]} missing." >&2
  if [ "${#resolve_lost[@]}" -gt 0 ]; then
    if [ -n "${A11Y_RESOLVE_REASON:-}" ]; then
      echo "pre-push: symbols main exports are missing here, overridden: $A11Y_RESOLVE_REASON" >&2
    else
      echo "pre-push: REFUSING — these are exported by origin/main and do not resolve after your merge:" >&2
      for missing in "${resolve_lost[@]}"; do echo "    $missing" >&2; done
      echo "That is what #232 looked like: a conflict resolved toward this branch removed four merged" >&2
      echo "units, source and tests together, and every check stayed green. Re-resolve taking main's side" >&2
      echo "for files you did not touch." >&2
      echo "If you are deliberately removing them: A11Y_RESOLVE_REASON=\"<why>\" git push ... — the" >&2
      echo "reason is printed, so a deliberate removal is in the log rather than in somebody's memory." >&2
      exit 1
    fi
  fi
else
  skipped+=("resolve-toward-main (origin/main not resolvable, or not an ancestor — see the refusal above)")
fi
# END B5 RESOLVE-TOWARD-MAIN

# #129: A CHECK CAN FAIL ON SOMEONE ELSE'S TREE. Three times on 2026-09-06 a refusal named the pusher's
# change when the real cause was another agent's mid-edit file, a stale export nobody here touched, or an
# untracked scratch file in a shared checkout -- every one read as "your push broke the build" and the
# documented response to that reading is A11Y_SKIP_VERIFY=1, which turns off lint, typecheck and every
# test as collateral. The hook already knows enough to say otherwise: `origin/main...HEAD` is exactly the
# files this push's own commits touch, and a failing check's own output already names the file it is
# unhappy about (eslint's `path:line`, tsc's, and node's own stack-trace shape all agree on it).
#
# `|| true` and the separate KNOWN flag matter: a git failure (no `origin/main` fetched, a fresh clone)
# must never be read as "this push touches nothing", which would make every failure look untouched and
# print a false reassurance. Silence, not a wrong note, is the safe failure mode here.
if PUSHED_FILES="$(git diff --name-only origin/main...HEAD 2>/dev/null)"; then
  PUSHED_KNOWN=true
else
  PUSHED_FILES=""
  PUSHED_KNOWN=false
fi
REPO_ROOT="$(pwd)"

# Best-effort, not a parser: a file this check's output never mentions cannot be attributed either way, so
# under-matching just loses the note, never produces a wrong one. Three shapes, because the three tools
# `run()` wraps do not agree: eslint prints the path ALONE on its own line, then `line:col` on the next;
# tsc prints `path(line,col): error ...`; node's own stack traces print `path:line:col`. A regex assuming
# only the last shape (tried first, against a synthetic eslint fixture) matched nothing.
mentioned_paths() {
  {
    grep -oE '^[[:space:]]*[A-Za-z0-9_./-]+\.(ts|tsx|mjs|js|py|sh|ya?ml|json)[[:space:]]*$' "$1" 2>/dev/null \
      | sed -E 's/^[[:space:]]+|[[:space:]]+$//g'
    grep -oE '[A-Za-z0-9_./-]+\.(ts|tsx|mjs|js)\([0-9]+,[0-9]+\)' "$1" 2>/dev/null \
      | sed -E 's/\([0-9]+,[0-9]+\)$//'
    grep -oE '([A-Za-z0-9_./-]+\.(ts|tsx|mjs|js|py|sh|ya?ml|json)):[0-9]+' "$1" 2>/dev/null \
      | sed -E 's/:[0-9]+$//'
  } 2>/dev/null | sed -E "s#^${REPO_ROOT}/##" | sort -u || true
}

# Prints nothing unless EVERY path the failure's own output named is absent from this push's files -- one
# path this push DOES touch is enough to withhold the note, because the failure may genuinely be the
# pusher's after all.
note_if_untouched() {
  local output_file="$1"
  [ "$PUSHED_KNOWN" = true ] || return 0
  local mentioned; mentioned="$(mentioned_paths "$output_file")"
  [ -n "$mentioned" ] || return 0
  local p
  for p in $mentioned; do
    # A here-string, not `printf | grep -q`: the same SIGPIPE-under-`pipefail` fault the B5 block above
    # records in full. It needs a >64 KB path list to bite here rather than a 73 KB source file, so it has
    # never been seen -- and "not yet reached" is not a different defect from "reached".
    grep -qxF "$p" <<< "$PUSHED_FILES" && return 0
  done
  echo "            NOTE: this failure is in file(s) this push does not touch — probably not your change:"
  echo "$mentioned" | sed 's/^/              /'
}

# A CHECK THAT COULD NOT RUN IS NOT A CHECK THAT FAILED, and until 2026-09-06 they printed the same.
#
# Pushing `main` from a worktree with no `node_modules` produced `3 check(s) failed: lint typecheck unit
# tests`, which reads exactly like the change breaking the build. The cause was `sh: eslint: command not
# found` — the gate examined NOTHING and reported three failures. That is this hook's own stated defect
# (see the comment above about "verified" coming to mean "unexamined") arriving in the runner rather than
# in a gate, and it is the condition under which somebody reaches for `A11Y_SKIP_VERIFY=1` and pushes
# something genuinely broken, because from the output there is no way to tell the two apart.
#
# So the toolchain is checked ONCE, before any check runs, and its absence STOPS the hook with a
# different message rather than being spread across three false failures. Refusing here is safe: a
# worktree cannot verify anything without its dependencies, so there is no honest push to allow through.
if [ ! -x node_modules/.bin/eslint ]; then
  echo "pre-push: REFUSING — no toolchain here (node_modules/.bin/eslint is missing)."
  echo "  This checkout cannot run lint, typecheck or the tests, so a push from it would be UNVERIFIED."
  echo "  It is not your change: nothing has been examined."
  echo "  Run \`npm install\` here, or push from a checkout that has one."
  exit 1
fi

run() { # run <label> <command...>
  local label="$1"; shift
  local status=0
  "$@" >/tmp/a11y-prepush.$$ 2>&1 || status=$?
  if [ "$status" -eq 0 ]; then
    echo "  ok      $label"
  elif [ "$status" -eq 127 ]; then
    # 127 is the shell's "command not found". The toolchain guard above catches the common cause; this
    # catches a check whose OWN binary is missing, and keeps it out of `failed` so the summary cannot
    # claim a verdict it did not reach.
    echo "  COULD NOT RUN  $label — a command it needs is not installed; nothing was examined"
    tail -5 /tmp/a11y-prepush.$$ | sed 's/^/            /'
    skipped+=("$label (could not run)")
  else
    echo "  FAILED  $label"
    tail -20 /tmp/a11y-prepush.$$ | sed 's/^/            /'
    note_if_untouched /tmp/a11y-prepush.$$
    failed+=("$label")
  fi
  rm -f /tmp/a11y-prepush.$$
}

# HOW THIS HOOK TIMES ITSELF.
#
# #911 removed the tree-wide sweep and with it the guards BUDGET (#704/#716) -- a ratio between the gate's
# CPU and the sweep's, which is a rule about a thing that no longer runs. The timing itself is kept,
# because a hook that cannot say what it cost is a hook nobody can argue about.
#
# WALL CLOCK ON THIS HOST MEASURES THE OTHER SESSIONS ON IT, NOT THIS HOOK'S WORK -- ceo's ruling,
# 2026-09-09, after three runs of the identical 21-guard sweep on one machine read 28.0s, 45.1s and 44.2s
# with no code change between them (#716). So the cost this hook REPORTS is CPU (`user`+`sys`), never
# wall: `times` reports the CUMULATIVE CPU this shell's finished children have consumed since the shell
# started, so a snapshot before and after a phase, subtracted, is that phase's own CPU cost -- immune to
# how many OTHER processes the host happened to be running at the moment, which is exactly what makes it
# usable as a budget rather than a mood.
#
# `times`, NEVER PIPED OR COMMAND-SUBSTITUTED -- `times | sed ...` or `x=$(times)` both fork bash into a
# SUBSHELL to satisfy the pipe/substitution, and a subshell's own `times` reports its OWN (near-zero)
# children, not this shell's accumulated ones. Measured directly: piped, the delta read 0.000 across a
# node loop that burned real CPU. Redirected to a FILE (`times > file`), no fork happens and the real
# cumulative figure survives to be read back. THE SAME CLASS AS #712 -- a status/value read from the wrong
# side of a pipe reports something real, just not the thing being asked about; that one was a piped exit
# code, this is a piped builtin's own accounting, but the shape (a fork silently substitutes its own
# state for the caller's) is identical.
#
# WALL CLOCK is still recorded, as a bound never the answer (ceo's own words) -- `date +%s.%N` is a GNU
# extension absent from a bare BSD `date`, so `node -e "Date.now()"` is the portable millisecond clock:
# node is already a hard dependency of every check this hook runs.
snapshot_children_cpu() { times > "$1"; }
children_cpu_seconds() {
  # $1: a file `snapshot_children_cpu` wrote. Line 2 of `times`' output is this shell's children,
  # cumulative since the shell started -- `0m12.340s 0m1.560s` (user, sys). `(tok+0)` in awk parses a
  # string's own leading numeric prefix and stops at the first non-digit, so the trailing "s" is silently
  # dropped rather than needing its own strip.
  sed -n '2p' "$1" | awk '{
    split($1, u, "m"); split($2, s, "m");
    user = u[1] * 60 + (u[2] + 0);
    sys  = s[1] * 60 + (s[2] + 0);
    printf "%.3f", user + sys
  }'
}
now_ms() { node -e "process.stdout.write(String(Date.now()))"; }
cpu_delta() { awk -v a="$1" -v b="$2" 'BEGIN{printf "%.3f", b - a}'; }
ms_delta_seconds() { awk -v a="$1" -v b="$2" 'BEGIN{printf "%.1f", (b - a) / 1000}'; }

# THE WHOLE GATE, and after #911 it is three checks: lint, typecheck and the leak scan. Each is a `run`
# call, which is the hook's own mechanism for "a check that can fail this push" --
# `pre-push-hook-scope.test.ts` enumerates those call sites from this file's text and refuses a fourth.
run_gate() { # run_gate <branch-label>
  local branch_label="$1"
  local cpu_start_file cpu_end_file wall_start wall_end
  cpu_start_file="/tmp/a11y-cpu-gate-start.$$"; cpu_end_file="/tmp/a11y-cpu-gate-end.$$"
  snapshot_children_cpu "$cpu_start_file"; wall_start="$(now_ms)"

  # LINT IS SCOPED BY CHANGED PATH, NOT BY CHANGED PACKAGE -- #911, measured rather than argued.
  # `changed-packages.mjs` lists `packages/<name>` directories only, so a `scripts/`-only change (the most
  # common shape of a row in this repository) scopes to the EMPTY LIST, and a check handed an empty
  # population reports clean about a population it never read. eslint's unit IS the file, so a path list
  # costs nothing in coverage. `changed-files.mjs` (#939) is the one place this repo asks git which paths a
  # range touched, and it lists BOTH SIDES of a rename -- a file moved out of a linted directory is still
  # linted here.
  #
  # THE COMMITTED DIFF **AND** THE DIRTY TREE, which is not the same list and the difference is a silent
  # hole. Every other note in this hook says the gate reads the TREE rather than the commits being pushed
  # -- that is why the dirty-tree notes at the bottom exist. A path list built from `origin/main...HEAD`
  # alone would lint exactly the files that ARE committed and skip the one you just edited, so the first
  # push of a freshly branched worktree would report `ok` having read nothing. Measured while writing
  # this: a new branch at origin/main with this very file edited scopes to ZERO committed paths.
  local lint_paths=()
  local seen_paths=""
  add_lint_path() { # add_lint_path <path> -- extension-filtered, existence-checked, de-duplicated
    case "$1" in *.ts|*.mjs|*.js|*.cjs) ;; *) return 0 ;; esac
    [ -f "$1" ] || return 0   # deleted, or the source side of a rename: nothing left on disk to lint
    case "$seen_paths" in *"|$1|"*) return 0 ;; esac
    seen_paths="$seen_paths|$1|"
    lint_paths+=("$1")
  }
  if git rev-parse --verify -q origin/main >/dev/null; then
    while IFS= read -r changed_path; do
      [ -n "$changed_path" ] && add_lint_path "$changed_path"
    done < <(node scripts/changed-files.mjs origin/main...HEAD)
  else
    # FAILS CLOSED, not open: with no base ref there is no committed diff to scope by, so read the whole
    # tree rather than read nothing and print "ok".
    add_lint_path "."
    lint_paths=(".")
    echo "pre-push: origin/main is not resolvable here, so lint reads the whole tree rather than a diff." >&2
  fi
  while IFS= read -r dirty_path; do
    [ -n "$dirty_path" ] && add_lint_path "$dirty_path"
  done < <(git status --porcelain | cut -c4-)
  if [ "${#lint_paths[@]}" -eq 0 ]; then
    skipped+=("lint (no .ts/.mjs/.js file changed against origin/main -- nothing for eslint to read)")
  else
    run "lint (${#lint_paths[@]} changed file(s))" npx eslint "${lint_paths[@]}"
  fi

  # TYPECHECK IS NOT SCOPED, AND THE REASON IS THE MECHANISM RATHER THAN A PREFERENCE -- #911.
  # tsc's unit is the PROGRAM, not the file. Measured on this checkout at 40e36ba4: the root program is
  # 953 files (534 of them `.test.ts`, 130 of them top-level `scripts/`); `tsc -p packages/<name>` is 0
  # test files, because every package tsconfig carries `"exclude": ["src/**/*.test.ts"]` so tests cannot
  # reach a consumer's node_modules; and no package program contains top-level `scripts/` at all. So
  # "typecheck the changed packages" drops 664 of the 953 and reports clean -- the root tsconfig's own
  # comment records that regression happening once already, 25 test files silently unchecked. It costs
  # 4.86s, against the 30.22s the tree-wide sweep cost before this row removed it.
  run "typecheck (the whole program)"  npm run --silent typecheck

  # THE LEAK SCAN IS THE ONE CHECK HERE WHOSE VALUE IS BEING BEFORE THE PUSH -- #911, and it is named by
  # file rather than swept for. Everything else this hook used to run is a second copy of CI minutes
  # later; a pushed branch on a public repository is visible the moment it lands, so this one cannot wait
  # for the merge. It was two of the 22 files in `guards:sweep`, which is why "keep the leak scan while
  # deleting the sweep" would have deleted it.
  run "leak scan" npx tsx --test \
    packages/lab/src/packaging/tracked-source-leak-guard.test.ts \
    packages/lab/src/packaging/tracked-prose-leak-guard.test.ts

  snapshot_children_cpu "$cpu_end_file"; wall_end="$(now_ms)"
  local gate_cpu gate_wall
  gate_cpu="$(cpu_delta "$(children_cpu_seconds "$cpu_start_file")" "$(children_cpu_seconds "$cpu_end_file")")"
  gate_wall="$(ms_delta_seconds "$wall_start" "$wall_end")"
  rm -f "$cpu_start_file" "$cpu_end_file"
  # A MEASURED NUMBER FROM THIS RUN, never a fixed figure quoted from a header -- CLAUDE.md's own rule.
  echo "pre-push: gate ($branch_label) -- wall ${gate_wall}s, CPU ${gate_cpu}s"
}

BRANCH="$(git rev-parse --abbrev-ref HEAD)"

# #386: PUSHING TO AN ARMED, GREEN PR RACES A MERGE THAT CAN COMPLETE UNDERNEATH IT. Measured three
# times in one evening, all by the person who wrote the rule against it -- each time the commit was
# correct, the push succeeded, and GitHub's auto-merge bot completed the merge in the window between the
# commit and the push. The commit then sits stranded on a branch nothing can find: `branches:stranded`
# correctly excludes it (the branch IS merged, not orphaned), and the tracker has no record of it either.
#
# A CONVENIENCE GUARD AGAINST A RACE, NOT A CORRECTNESS GATE -- fails open, loudly, on anything that
# stops it asking: no `gh`, no auth, no network, no PR yet. Never refuses a push for a reason unrelated
# to the one specific window this exists to close (armed AND already green).
#
# `main` never has a PR against itself, so this is skipped there rather than paying for a lookup that
# can only ever answer "no PR".
# BEGIN #386 ARMED-PR PUSH GUARD -- extraction markers for pre-push-armed-pr.test.ts; keep both if edited.
if [ "$BRANCH" != "main" ]; then
  if ! command -v gh >/dev/null 2>&1; then
    skipped+=("armed-pr-push-guard (no gh on PATH -- could not ask, allowing)")
  elif [ -n "${A11Y_ALLOW_ARMED_PUSH:-}" ]; then
    echo "pre-push: armed-pr-push-guard overridden: $A11Y_ALLOW_ARMED_PUSH" >&2
  else
    # `if VAR=$(cmd); then ... else ...; fi`, NEVER a bare `VAR=$(cmd); CODE=$?` -- under `set -e` a plain
    # assignment statement IS a simple command, so a non-zero command substitution inside one aborts the
    # script on the spot, before the next line (`CODE=$?`) ever runs. Wrapping it as an `if` condition is
    # one of the few contexts `set -e` exempts, which is the ONLY reason this can read the exit code at
    # all rather than dying silently with no message printed -- found by this guard's own wiring test,
    # not by reasoning about it.
    if ARMED_CHECK_OUT="$(node scripts/merge-guard.mjs --armed-check="$BRANCH" 2>&1)"; then
      ARMED_CHECK_CODE=0
    else
      ARMED_CHECK_CODE=$?
    fi
    if [ "$ARMED_CHECK_CODE" -ne 0 ]; then
      echo "$ARMED_CHECK_OUT" >&2
      echo "If this is deliberate: A11Y_ALLOW_ARMED_PUSH=\"<why>\" git push ... -- the reason is printed," >&2
      echo "not just the override, so a deliberate exception is in the log rather than in somebody's memory." >&2
      exit 1
    fi
    if [ -n "$ARMED_CHECK_OUT" ]; then
      echo "pre-push: $ARMED_CHECK_OUT" >&2
    fi
  fi
fi
# END #386 ARMED-PR PUSH GUARD

# ONE PATH, NOT THREE -- #911. This used to branch: `main` ran the gate plus the corpus checks, a
# BOARD-ONLY diff ran the board guards INSTEAD of lint and typecheck, and every other branch ran the gate
# plus a changeset gate. The three paths were a way of paying less on the diffs that needed less, and with
# the sweep gone the gate is small enough that the branching cost more than it saved -- in correctness,
# not in seconds.
#
# AND THE BOARD-ONLY PATH WAS A HOLE THIS ROW CLOSES BY ACCIDENT, which is worth naming rather than
# leaving to be re-found: it never ran `guards:sweep`, so it never ran the leak guards either. A push
# whose whole diff is `docs/board/summaries/*.md` is PROSE, which is exactly the population
# `tracked-prose-leak-guard.test.ts` reads -- the one kind of diff the leak scan was skipped for was the
# one kind made entirely of the text it scans. Now every push runs it.
#
# A board-only diff still pays almost nothing: lint is scoped by changed path and a `.md`-only diff has
# no lintable file in it, so lint reports SKIPPED with its reason and only typecheck and the leak scan run.
run_gate "$BRANCH"

# WHAT THIS HOOK DOES NOT RUN, SAID UNCONDITIONALLY -- #911.
#
# `training:check-signals` and `rules:gate` read the corpus in gitignored `runs/`, and the 2026-09-06
# ruling is that a gate reading `runs/` gives a VERDICT only when the agent driving the fleet and the lab
# runs it. Anyone else may run one as a PRE-CHECK, never as a reported result. In this hook it could only
# ever have been a pre-check, so it is not run here at all.
#
# UNCONDITIONAL, where it used to depend on whether `runs/` happened to be present. That is the one
# difference that matters: the conditional form was SILENT on the machine that HAS a copy of `runs/`, and
# a stale copy is precisely the machine that most needs telling -- one measured here was 89 hours old and
# carried neither of the two keys a sweep across it was written to find, so it reported cleanly having
# examined a corpus that no longer exists.
# BEGIN #911 NOT-RUN LINE -- extraction markers for pre-push-hook-scope.test.ts; keep both if edited.
echo "  NOT RUN training:check-signals, rules:gate -- both read the corpus in runs/, and a copy here is"
echo "          only as fresh as its last sync. The authoritative verdict comes from the lab:"
echo "          npm run lab:job -- -e job=check-signals   |   npm run lab:job -- -e job=rules-gate"
# END #911 NOT-RUN LINE


for s in ${skipped+"${skipped[@]}"}; do echo "  SKIPPED $s"; done

if [ ${#failed[@]} -gt 0 ]; then
  echo "" >&2
  echo "pre-push: ${#failed[@]} check(s) failed: ${failed[*]}" >&2
  # #721 (`lead/scored-criteria-v19` at ae5d2c03, re-derived rather than merged -- that branch's own copy
  # of the line below predates #706's A11Y_SKIP_VERIFY_REASON requirement, so rescue:hunk correctly
  # refused to overwrite it): THE GATE READS THE WORKING TREE, NOT THE COMMITS BEING PUSHED -- and in a
  # SHARED checkout those are different things. Measured 2026-09-06: a push from the primary was refused
  # with `1391 problems (2 errors)`, and `npm run lint` re-run immediately afterwards exited 0 with none.
  # The errors were another agent's in-flight edit, present when the gate ran and gone by the time anyone
  # looked. The pusher had not created that state and could not reproduce it.
  #
  # That is worse than a plain false failure because it is NOT REPRODUCIBLE: re-running gives a clean
  # result, which reads as flakiness, and a flaky gate is the one people learn to push past. So a refusal
  # NAMES the possibility rather than leaving the reader to guess -- this cannot make the gate correct,
  # and it makes an unattributable refusal attributable, which is what stops A11Y_SKIP_VERIFY=1 becoming
  # the habit.
  #
  # BEGIN #721 DIRTY-TREE NOTE -- extraction markers for pre-push-dirty-tree-note.test.ts; keep both if edited.
  # sed, not one of the three STATUS_TOOLS -- #375's piped-exit-status guard scopes its hazard check to
  # top-level code as ONE bucket, and this file's top level legitimately reads $? elsewhere
  # (#911 removed the three that did; the shape is kept because the guard reads this file as one bucket). This line never
  # reads $? at all -- the whole pipeline is captured into `dirty` -- but the guard cannot see that, so a
  # truncation pipeline ending in one of its three flagged tools reads as the same hazard those other
  # reads exist to avoid. Truncating with sed's own line-range form sidesteps the collision entirely.
  dirty="$(git status --porcelain 2>/dev/null | sed -n '1,20p')"
  if [ -n "$dirty" ]; then
    echo "" >&2
    echo "NOTE: the working tree was NOT CLEAN when these checks ran, and the gate reads the TREE" >&2
    echo "rather than the commits you are pushing. In a shared checkout some of this may not be yours:" >&2
    echo "$dirty" | sed 's/^/    /' >&2
    echo "  Re-run the failing check before believing it. If it passes, the gate saw somebody else's" >&2
    echo "  half-finished edit and this refusal is not about your commits." >&2
  fi
  # END #721 DIRTY-TREE NOTE
  echo "Fix them, or push anyway with:  A11Y_SKIP_VERIFY_REASON=\"<why>\" A11Y_SKIP_VERIFY=1 git push ..." >&2
  exit 1
fi

# BEGIN #760 DIRTY-TREE PASS NOTE -- extraction markers for pre-push-dirty-tree-note.test.ts; keep both if edited.
# #721's note above only prints on FAILURE -- a PASS on a dirty tree said nothing, the dangerous direction:
# nothing follows a false pass. Measured twice in one day. worker-judge (#748) rewrote a test file's
# fixtures, ran the suite against the working tree (passed), then `fetch`/`merge`/`push` WITHOUT staging
# the edit -- incoming commits never touched that file, so the merge was clean and left the rewrite
# uncommitted, and every commit and push since carried the ORIGINAL tests. dispatcher hit the identical
# shape the same morning: a fix to `trunk-revert-guard.test.ts` sat uncommitted while three other files
# were staged and pushed. Both local runs passed; neither push carried what was verified. Worker-judge's
# own sentence for it: "a local pass proves the working tree, never proves what got pushed."
pass_dirty="$(git status --porcelain 2>/dev/null | sed -n '1,20p')"
if [ -n "$pass_dirty" ]; then
  echo "" >&2
  echo "NOTE: the working tree was NOT CLEAN, and these checks read the TREE, not the commits being" >&2
  echo "pushed. Uncommitted, and therefore NOT in this push:" >&2
  echo "$pass_dirty" | sed 's/^/    /' >&2
  # LOUDER when a file is BOTH dirty right now AND in the push's own diff -- worker-judge's #748 case
  # precisely: verified in one state, pushed in another. `push_remote_sha`/`push_local_sha` come from the
  # stdin loop above, read once for this whole hook; skipped on a brand-new branch (`push_remote_sha` all
  # zeros, no prior remote state to diff against) since the general note above already covers that case.
  if [ -n "${push_remote_sha:-}" ] && [ "$push_remote_sha" != "0000000000000000000000000000000000000000" ] \
    && [ -n "${push_local_sha:-}" ]; then
    dirty_paths="$(echo "$pass_dirty" | cut -c4-)"
    pushed_paths="$(git diff --name-only "$push_remote_sha".."$push_local_sha" 2>/dev/null)"
    both_dirty_and_pushed="$(comm -12 <(printf '%s\n' "$dirty_paths" | sort -u) \
      <(printf '%s\n' "$pushed_paths" | sort -u) 2>/dev/null)"
    if [ -n "$both_dirty_and_pushed" ]; then
      echo "" >&2
      echo "  LOUDER: the file(s) below are BOTH uncommitted right now AND changed by the commit(s) you" >&2
      echo "  are pushing -- verified in one state, pushed in another:" >&2
      echo "$both_dirty_and_pushed" | sed 's/^/      /' >&2
    fi
  fi
  echo "  A local pass proves the working tree, never what got pushed." >&2
fi
# END #760 DIRTY-TREE PASS NOTE

echo "pre-push: ok${skipped+ (with ${#skipped[@]} skipped, listed above)}"
